{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790527957,"nonce":"qcI0zpzCVdBfjHnXbbYPJzwnEEY=","title":"Windows: with the compile worker on, a tuple-returning function aborts at exit and a Lua loop reads a number as userdata","message":"Two faults seen only on native tests (x86_64-pc-windows-msvc), only with warm-up on (the compile worker replacing quick compiles with optimised ones), never on Linux or Intel macOS:\n\n1. Python conformance exceptions/try_finally_tuple_return.py (warm_up::exceptions) prints the right output and then exits 0xC0000409, on CI runs 36327995246 (6bb5a3c7) and 36328181956 (3b26b345), so it reproduces. 0xC0000409 is the fast-fail a failed stack-cookie check raises, and a Rust abort too. The case's functions return tuples (value structs); a disagreement between the quick compile and the optimised recompile, or between either and its caller, on how an aggregate is returned under the Windows x64 ABI (hidden pointer vs registers) would corrupt the frame this way.\n\n2. Lua official/math.lua (warm_up::official) on run 36331461720 (69e9ca8f): math.lua:902 `up = max(up, t)` in the full-integer random test raises \"attempt to compare number with userdata\". The same case passed on Windows at 3b26b345 and 17c83ed7, so it depends on timing or on the random seed; 30 Linux runs with warm-up on pass, including the float test's retry path the failing run took.\n\nNeither case's stderr names the fault yet; Python conformance now prints the last lines of a failing case's stderr (69e9ca8f on fix/ci-platform-flakes), so the next Windows failure of case 1 should. Pinning either down needs a Windows machine: run the case with warm-up on and ZYNTAX_BASELINE_THRESHOLD=1, then with ZYNTAX_DISABLE_WARM_UP=1 to confirm the worker is required, and compare the signatures the two compiles of the tuple-returning function are built with.","files":null}]}